# nhs-ae-forecast

**Does backtesting on revised official statistics overstate forecast skill?** For NHS
England's monthly A&E series the answer, measured here, is no: with every version of
every file since 2015 recovered, forecasts trained on the data as first published score
within 0.6% of forecasts trained on the revised data, for four models and three targets,
and the model ranking does not change. Late submissions matter more than revisions, and
neither matters much. That is a method (a vintage archive and an as-of harness) plus a
calibrated expectation that transfers to any series revised after publication.

Around that result: probabilistic, hierarchical forecasts of A&E attendances and
emergency admissions at provider level, and a winter bed-escalation decision layer that
turns them into something a planning lead can act on.

The parts that consulting clients actually pay for and portfolios usually skip:

- **Vintage archive.** NHS England revises monthly files twice a year. Every file
  version is stored under the date it became current, so backtests can use only what
  was known at forecast time. Versions from before this archive existed are recovered
  from the Internet Archive's captures of the NHS England year pages (`recover`).
  Pre-registered hypothesis H4 tests how much the usual practice (backtesting on final
  data) overstates skill.
- **Baselines first.** Seasonal naive, ETS and STL+ARIMA are the bar; LightGBM and a
  hierarchical PyMC model have to beat them in as-of mode.
- **Coherent uncertainty.** Quantile forecasts reconciled provider → ICB → region →
  England; calibration reported, not assumed.
- **A decision, in beds.** Admissions forecast → bed-days → probability of exceeding 92%
  occupancy → escalation beds required, per trust, per winter month.
- **Runs without me.** Monthly GitHub Action, scorecard page, handover pack.

Status: **week 1 — ingestion and vintage archive.** See `docs/scope.md` for the plan and
`docs/preregistration.md` for what will be tested and how.

## Quick start

```bash
git clone <this repo> && cd nhs-ae-forecast
make install                                  # pip install -e ".[dev,xls,stats,gbm]"
make test                                     # 82 unit tests, no network needed

nhs-ae-ingest discover --years 2026-27        # list the monthly files on the NHS England page
nhs-ae-ingest run                             # archive today's vintage of every year, build parquet
nhs-ae-ingest recover                         # recover historical vintages via the Internet Archive
nhs-ae-ingest coverage --origins 2019-09 2025-09   # origins lacking an as-of version (Appendix A)
nhs-ae-ingest build                           # rebuild parquet from the archive, offline
nhs-ae-backtest run --models b0 b1 b2 m1 --jobs 8   # rolling-origin backtest, as-of and final modes
nhs-ae-backtest audit                         # what revisions changed; as-of vs final training data
```

After `run`, you have:

```
data/raw/2026-08-13/July-2026-CSV-Trf28h.csv     # raw file, as published, immutable
data/raw/manifest.jsonl                          # one line per version: url, period, sha256,
                                                 #   available_from (+ how it was estimated)
data/processed/ae_monthly_all_vintages.parquet   # tidy long table, every vintage
data/processed/ae_monthly_latest.parquet         # most recent value per (period, org, metric)
```

Tidy long schema: `period, org_code, parent_org, org_name, metric, value, is_total,
source_file, snapshot, revised, revised_on, sha256`. Metric names are canonical
(`att_type1`, `adm_via_ae_type1`, …; full list in `src/nhs_ae/config.py`). The parser
reads all three physical layouts NHS England has used: the modern CSV (2018-04 on), the
workbooks with a title block and a two-row header (the only format before 2018-04, and
the XLS copy of every month since), and the February/March 2018 split CSVs. Header
drift is handled by regex rules on the joined group + sub-header text; derivable
columns (totals, percentages) are dropped, anything unrecognised is kept as
`unmapped__<name>` and logged, and a file with no recognised metric at all is rejected
(NHS England once linked a growth-rates workbook as a monthly file). Files the
reader cannot open are listed in `data/processed/parse_failures.csv`.

## Repository layout

```
src/nhs_ae/
  config.py         source URLs, canonical schema, header-mapping rules
  ingest/           discover → download (vintage archive) → recover (history) → parse → lineage; CLI
  features/         calendar, weather, surveillance, regimes, hierarchy   (weeks 3–4)
  features/hierarchy.py  provider → current region → England (ICB needs the mapping file)
  models/           base.py contract; B0 ✔ B1 ETS ✔ B2 STL+ARIMA ✔ M1 LightGBM+conformal ✔; PyMC to come
  reconcile/        MinT and sample-path reconciliation                    (week 5)
  evaluate/         as-of loader ✔, metrics ✔, rolling-origin harness ✔, CLI ✔
  monitor/          error tracking, drift triggers                         (week 7)
  decide/           bed-days, breach probability, escalation beds         (week 6)
  app/              Streamlit                                              (week 7)
docs/
  scope.md          what is in and out, data sources, mess handling, plan, risks
  preregistration.md hypotheses, metrics, splits, decision rules — frozen by tag
  handover.md       operational runbook (skeleton, completed week 7)
  memo_template.md  one-page output for a winter-planning lead
  results.md        pre-registered tests as they are evaluated (H1, H2, H4 so far)
tests/              unit tests on a SYNTHETIC fixture (no real NHS numbers)
.github/workflows/  monthly ingest on the second-Thursday window
data/raw/           committed vintage archive (CSV only; XLS ignored)
```

Each stage's `__init__.py` documents what it will contain and the rule it must obey
(for example, features may only use values published on or before the forecast origin).

## Backtesting

`nhs-ae-backtest run` walks monthly origins (2019-09 to 2025-09 by default). At origin
month *M* the forecaster is taken to run on the second Thursday of *M*, just after the
file for *M−1* is published, so training data end at *M−1* and horizon *h* targets
*M−1+h*: an October origin forecasts December to March at horizons 3 to 6. As-of mode
uses the archived version of every period that was current on that day; final mode uses
the latest revision of the same periods. Both modes run through one code path, every
model writes the same long quantile table, and scores go to `data/processed/backtest/`.

## Data

NHS England, *A&E Attendances and Emergency Admissions*, monthly provider-level files,
June 2015 onward, Open Government Licence v3.0. Files are published on the second
Thursday of each month and revised in May and November. Provider-to-ICB mapping is
published with each release; trust mergers are resolved via the ODS organisation API.

Revisions get a new URL and the original stays on the server (verified 2017–2024), so
`recover` rebuilds the version history from Internet Archive captures of the year pages
and fetches the files themselves from NHS England. Files from 2015-16 to 2018-19 *were*
replaced in place, so for those the Archive's own file captures are the only source of
pre-revision bytes; `coverage` reports what could not be reconstructed.

Known issues the pipeline is built around: missing providers each month (stored as NaN,
never zero), revisions (vintage archive), Type 3 → UTC reclassification and
booked appointments from August 2020 (regime flags), fourteen field-testing trusts
2019–23, the COVID break, and a May 2025 errata on provider-level admissions.

## Roadmap

| Week | Milestone |
|---|---|
| 1 | Ingestion, vintage archive, lineage, historical vintage recovery ✔ |
| 2 | As-of loader, harness, metrics, B0/B1/B2 ✔; revision audit; freeze `prereg-v1` |
| 3–4 | LightGBM ✔ (2026-09-09); PyMC time-boxed |
| 5 | Reconciliation, cold-start evaluation |
| 6 | Decision layer, memo |
| 7 | Monitoring, forecast Action, Pages scorecard, Streamlit, handover |
| 8 | Write-up; first live forecast for Dec 2026–Mar 2027 by 31 Oct 2026 |
| Phase 2 | Allocation of escalation beds across trusts (stochastic programme); winter SitRep at daily resolution |

